Smart homes are increasingly relying on motion detection technology to identify occupants and track their activities. However, conventional motion sensors, such as Passive Infrared devices, often fail to provide accurate results due to their limited field of view and sensitivity to changes in temperature and lighting levels. In this study, the performance of modern motion sensing technology, including channel state information from Wi-Fi and passive infrared sensors, is evaluated and compared in various scenarios. The results show that channel state information from Wi-Fi signals can provide reliable movement detection and tracking in different smart home settings, including individual and shared living spaces. Furthermore, the findings indicate that channel state information can effectively track an individual's activities, including entering and exiting a room. In contrast, passive infrared sensors struggled to provide consistent movement detection and tracking in various scenarios. The study also explores the limitations of channel state information in tracking multiple occupants and its sensitivity to room size, Wi-Fi access point placement, and the number of individuals involved. While channel state information offers potential for reliable motion detection and tracking in smart homes, it faces challenges in its inability to simultaneously estimate the location and identity of multiple occupants. Future research will focus on addressing these challenges to enhance the effectiveness of channel state information in smart home settings. The source code for this study is available on GitHub at https://github.com/ashishkpatel/MotionDetection. The corresponding author can be contacted via email at aapatwari@kth.se. The authors are grateful to the Swedish Foundation for Strategic Environmental Research for their funding support through ProCoast and to the Horizon 2020 project DREAM for its funding support through both the above mentioned projects. The authors also ack